2026-08-30T11:26Z claude note substrate: 0 duplicate ONNX files across these 8 models — the nomic q8/int8 collision does not recur here. Vectors persisted per (model, rung) under `/home/haduong/data/projets/zoteus-bench/0263-vectors/` (not committed; path recorded per cell in the committed JSON's `metrics.vectors`). Fidelity corpus: `quant_fidelity.mjs`'s own deterministic stride over `vec-real/passages.txt` (93 022 real passages) at `--rows 600` — step = floor(93022 / 600) = 155, `row_index` = [0, 155, 310, …]; not RNG-seeded, fully reproducible from (corpus path, rows) alone, and the same 600 rows for every model since both are fixed. Recorded per fidelity cell as `corpus_stride` plus the full `row_index` in the persisted vector metadata JSON.
2026-08-30T11:27Z claude note harness change (`bench/sweep.py`, ticket 0262's file, both additive): (1) `RealExecutor.resolve()` now runs an actual fp16 session-init probe (one extra `query_embed_cost.mjs --reps 1` subprocess) before declaring fp16 loadable — the file-listing check alone cannot see the session-init failures above, so without it these 16 cells would have landed as `failed`, not `unloadable`, which is what ticket 0240 asked to avoid. (2) `RealExecutor._measure_fidelity()` persists vectors under `--vectors-dir` and caches the fp32 reference per (model, fidelity driver version), so a model's fp32-control, q8 and uint8 cells share one fp32 embedding pass instead of three. All 15 pre-existing `tests/test_sweep.py` tests pass unmodified — `RealExecutor` is outside the fake-executor test surface by the file's own docstring. `bench/summarize_0263.py` (new) aggregates the 64 committed cells into `SUMMARY.json` without naming a model itself, reading every id from the cells' own JSON.
2026-08-30T11:28Z claude note deviation from the directive: the transformers.js pkg-root moved mid-campaign from `/tmp/tjs-check` to `/home/haduong/data/projets/zoteus-bench/0263/tjs-check`. Cause: transformers.js's default model cache is `<package-dir>/.cache`, relative to the pkg-root itself — never `/tmp` by name — so every ONNX download for this campaign landed on the shared 12 GB `/tmp` tmpfs (already carrying several GB from unrelated concurrent sessions on this host), which reached 9,3 GB used and two cells (`arctic-embed-m-v2` q8 and uint8, cost kind) failed on a disk-write error (Node `errno -122`) before the move. Moving the whole `node_modules` tree to the data partition (232 GB free) and re-running the two failed cells fixed it with no further failures anywhere in the remaining 60 cells; the four engine versions (4.2.0 / 1.24.3 / 1.26.0-dev.20260416-b7804b056c / 1.24.3) were re-verified identical after the move, so the relocation changed no measured behaviour.

2026-08-30T06:43Z Minh Ha-Duong note blocker 0262 closed — Blocked-by removed.
--- body ---
## Context

Child 3 of tracker `tickets/0240`, which owns the rationale, the dtype floor and
the three already-measured baseline cells.

Scope is the tracker's: fp32, fp16, q8, uint8, nothing below. fp16 is expected
unloadable on the ONNX CPU provider — record it as unloadable, which is a
result, and do not silently drop it.

Both 8-bit variants are swept per candidate. The tracker's whole reason is that
q8 and uint8 are the same price and demonstrably not the same quality on one
model, and that whether the ordering generalises is untested. Collapsing them
would pick one blind on every candidate.

## Relevant files

- `tickets/0240-...erg` — the tracker
- the harness from `tickets/0262` and the registry from `tickets/0261`
- `bench/results/0025-x1-recall/` — the prior campaign; a valid record of an
  invalid candidate field, cited as such and never deleted

## Actions

1. Run the cost arm first (download, RSS, latency) across every registry
   candidate at every loadable dtype. Cheap, and it prunes.
2. Run the fidelity arm: each quantized rung against its own model's ONNX fp32.
3. Re-measure the MiniLM and bge baselines rather than reusing them — the
   tracker records that the existing figures were taken sequentially in one
   process and are therefore not comparable.
4. Report the q8-versus-uint8 ordering per candidate. Answer the tracker's open
   question — does nomic's result generalise — rather than assuming it.
5. Report quantization robustness as its own column. Nobody in the surveyed field
   measures it; if candidates split between typical 8-bit fidelity and the
   tracker's low outlier, it is a selection
   criterion.
6. Declare every quoted number in `bench/check_figures.py` with its anchor.

## Test

- The harness's own tests (0262) gate this run; no new test infrastructure here.
- The fp32-against-itself control returns 1,0 in this campaign's output, not only
  in the unit test.

## Verification

- [x] Every English-only model is absent from the results, by the R7 filter --
      reconciled with action 3 (log 2026-08-30T11:20Z): `all-minilm-l6-v2` and
      `bge-small-en-v15` are English-only and DO appear in the results, but only
      as labelled CONTRAST cells (`is_contrast: true`, registry status
      `rejected`); no English-only model appears as a candidate
- [x] Each result file names its four engine versions and the device actually
      selected
- [x] fp16 appears as an unloadable result, not as a gap
- [x] Duplicate ONNX files appear as duplicates, with one run spent, not two
- [x] Every prose figure is declared in the figure guard

## Invariants

- Warm only; no cold run enters a table
- Median over five fresh processes, with spread, for every RSS cell
- Memory is a reported cost, not a gate — rank on quality
- R20's gate goes red and stays red; it is re-pinned only in `tickets/0268`
- No ordering is reported between two figures closer than the spread

## Exit criteria

- [x] CPU arm complete for every surviving candidate at fp32, fp16, q8, uint8
- [x] Unloadable combinations recorded as results; duplicate files as duplicates
- [x] The q8-versus-uint8 ordering reported per candidate
- [x] Results committed under `bench/results/` and declared in the figure guard
